WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,299
Defenders usually describe DDoS events in three broad categories because each category points to different signals, owners, and controls.

2
00:00:09,299 --> 00:00:12,815
Volumetric events are about scale against capacity.

3
00:00:12,815 --> 00:00:19,066
They attempt to consume available bandwidth, edge throughput, or provider-facing capacity.

4
00:00:19,066 --> 00:00:27,740
From a defensive perspective, the important question is whether traffic is saturating the path before it reaches the application.

5
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:33,679
If the pipe, edge, or provider handoff is overwhelmed, local server tuning may not matter.

6
00:00:33,679 --> 00:00:40,008
The response usually requires upstream visibility, edge protection, or provider assistance.

7
00:00:40,008 --> 00:00:46,884
Protocol or state exhaustion events target the ability of infrastructure to track and process traffic.

8
00:00:46,884 --> 00:00:54,230
The affected component might be a firewall, load balancer, connection table, proxy, or other stateful device.

9
00:00:54,230 --> 00:01:03,138
The defensive question is whether connection rates, half-open sessions, table pressure, or device saturation are limiting service.

10
00:01:03,138 --> 00:01:12,046
Controls in this category must be careful because they can affect legitimate users, monitoring, partner systems, and recovery paths.

11
00:01:12,046 --> 00:01:16,422
The right control is usually scoped, observable, and reversible.

12
00:01:16,422 --> 00:01:23,220
Application-layer events are different because the traffic may look closer to ordinary web or API use.

13
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:33,925
The pressure appears in expensive routes, login flows, search features, dynamic pages, payment paths, or APIs that require database, cache, or downstream work.

14
00:01:33,925 --> 00:01:42,130
The network may not look saturated, but latency, errors, queue depth, CPU, memory, database load, or thread pools can climb.

15
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:52,366
Application-layer defense often needs security, SRE, platform, and application teams together because the best control may be a WAF rule, a cache change,

16
00:01:52,366 --> 00:01:56,117
a quota, a circuit breaker, or a temporary degraded mode.

17
00:01:56,117 --> 00:01:59,868
Classification is not about assigning a dramatic label.

18
00:01:59,868 --> 00:02:02,525
It is about choosing the right next move.

19
00:02:02,525 --> 00:02:05,807
A volumetric event may need provider escalation.

20
00:02:05,807 --> 00:02:10,339
A state exhaustion event may need connection and upstream controls.

21
00:02:10,339 --> 00:02:16,668
An application-layer event may need route-specific protection and business prioritization.

22
00:02:16,668 --> 00:02:19,090
Good defenders avoid overclaiming.

23
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:28,155
They describe what they can observe, state the confidence level, and keep the response focused on availability, recovery, and evidence.
